## Frequently Asked Questions

> **Answer Box:**
> The most important audit software questions concern workflow fit, field usability, evidence, corrective actions, reporting, permissions, onboarding, security, scalability, integrations, and cost. The right answers depend on how your organization conducts audits, how many locations it manages, and what happens after findings are identified.

### What features should audit management software have?

Core capabilities can include scheduling, checklist management, mobile audits, evidence capture, findings, corrective actions, reporting, dashboards, permissions, audit history, notifications, integrations, and administration.

### How do I evaluate audit management software?

Start with your current audit workflow. Identify where scheduling, fieldwork, evidence, follow-up, reporting, or administration creates problems. Then test vendors using your real checklist, users, locations, and scenarios.

### What should I ask during an audit software demo?

Ask the vendor to demonstrate your actual workflow: scheduling an audit, assigning it, completing it in the field, capturing evidence, recording a failure, assigning a corrective action, resolving it, and reporting the result.

### How important is mobile support?

Mobile support can be critical when audits happen at physical locations. Test whether field users can complete the actual workflow efficiently on the devices they will use.

### What should audit software do with failed findings?

A useful audit workflow should connect failed findings with responsibility and follow-up. Depending on the organization, that may include an owner, deadline, status, escalation, and evidence of resolution.

### How important is evidence capture?

Evidence can make audit findings easier to review and verify by preserving supporting information such as photographs, notes, timestamps, or location information where appropriate.

### How should I compare audit software pricing?

Compare total cost rather than the advertised subscription alone. Consider users, locations, implementation, training, migration, storage, integrations, support, usage limits, and the expected operational benefit.

### How do I know whether audit software is easy to use?

Test it with a real field user completing a real audit. Observe how easily they find the audit, answer questions, capture evidence, record failures, save progress, and submit the result.

### What security questions should I ask an audit software vendor?

Ask about authentication, access controls, data protection, backups, retention, permissions, audit history, and available security documentation. Match those questions to your organization's data and regulatory requirements.

### How do I test audit software before buying?

Use your own audit template, location structure, user roles, field scenario, failed finding, corrective action, and reporting requirements. Run the complete process from scheduling through resolution.

### How do I know whether audit software is working after implementation?

Measure adoption, audit completion, overdue work, corrective-action closure, repeat findings, reporting effort, and the time spent coordinating audits. Compare these measures with the process you had before implementation.

